.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines read and write JSON (RFC 8259) the way Go's
.B encoding/json
package does: a decoder gives a stream's tokens, or decodes an object into a struct by a table
of its fields, or gives a value's text as it came; an encoder writes values to a
.IR Fmt
(see
.IR fmtinstall (2)),
with the commas and colons between them.
A number is its text, in both directions: what it means is for the program to say.
.SS Decoding
.I Jsondec
decodes what the Biobuf
.I b
reads;
.I jsondecmem
decodes the
.I n
bytes at
.IR s .
The input may hold any number of values, one after another, with white space between them.
.I Jsondecfree
frees a decoder.
.PP
.I Jsontoken
reads the next token into
.IR t ,
as Go's
.B Decoder.Token
does: the commas and colons are the decoder's, and do not come out.
.I T->type
is
.BR '{' ,
.BR '}' ,
.BR '[' ,
.BR ']' ,
.BR JSONnull ,
.BR JSONbool
.RI ( t->b
is its value),
.BR JSONnumber ,
or
.BR JSONstring .
For a number,
.I t->s
is its text; for a string, its decoded bytes, which may hold a NUL
.RB ( \eu0000 ),
and
.I t->n
is their length.
Both stay until the next call.
An object's keys come as strings.
.I Jsontoken
returns 1, or 0 at the end of the input between values, or \-1 on an error.
.PP
.I Jsonmore
is whether the array or object being read has another element (Go's
.BR Decoder.More ).
.I Jsonskip
reads the next value and drops it.
.I Jsonraw
reads the next value and gives its text, byte for byte as it came, in
.IR r ,
as Go's
.BR json.RawMessage :
.I r->s
is malloc'd, and NUL-terminated, and
.I r->n
is its length.
.PP
.I Jsondecode
reads an object into the struct at
.IR v ,
by the table
.IR f ,
whose last entry has a nil
.IR name .
An entry gives a key's
.IR name ,
the
.I kind
of its member, and the member's offset
.RI ( off ,
from
.BR offsetof ).
The kinds are
.B Jstring
(a
.B char*
to the decoded string),
.B Jint
.RB ( vlong ),
.B Jfloat
.RB ( double ),
.B Jnumber
(a
.B char*
to the number's text),
.B Jbool
.RB ( int ),
.B Jraw
(a
.BR Jsonraw ),
.B Jobject
(a struct, by the table
.IR sub ),
and
.BR Jfunc ,
for which
.I fn
is called with the decoder and the member's address to read the value itself:
an array, say, with
.I jsontoken
and
.IR jsonmore .
Strings are malloc'd.
As in Go, a key that no entry names is skipped, and
.B null
leaves a member as it was; unlike Go, a key matches only a name that is the same.
An object that is
.B null
leaves all of
.I v
as it was.
.PP
Escapes in strings are decoded as 9front's
.IR json (2)
decodes them; a
.B \eu
escape of a surrogate pair is one rune, and a surrogate alone is U+FFFD, as in Go.
The first error stays: every call after it fails with it.
.SS Encoding
.I Jsonenc
starts the encoder
.I e
on
.IR f ,
which
.IR fmtstrinit ,
.I fmtfdinit
or a
.I %
verb's own
.I Fmt
has made ready.
.I Jsonbegin
opens an object
.RB ( '{' )
or an array
.RB ( '[' );
.I jsonend
closes what was opened last.
In an object,
.I jsonkey
comes before each value.
.IR Jsonstring ,
.IR jsonstringn ,
.IR jsonnumber
(a number's text, as it is),
.IR jsonint ,
.IR jsonbool ,
.I jsonnull
and
.I jsonrawval
(a value's text, as it is: a
.IR Jsonraw 's,
given back)
write values.
Values written one after another outside of any container are on lines of their own, as Go's
.B Encoder
writes them.
.I Jsonquote
writes the
.I n
bytes at
.I s
to
.I f
as a JSON string, with 9front's escapes; a byte that is not UTF-8 becomes
.BR \eufffd .
.SH EXAMPLE
Read the
.B type
and
.B usage
of an event of Anthropic's Messages API, and write a request.
.IP
.EX
typedef struct Ev Ev;
struct Ev {
	char *type;
	vlong out;
};
Jsonfield usage[] = {
	{"output_tokens", Jint, offsetof(Ev, out)},
	{nil},
};
Jsonfield ev[] = {
	{"type", Jstring, offsetof(Ev, type)},
	{"usage", Jobject, 0, usage},
	{nil},
};
.EE
.IP
.EX
d = jsondecmem(data, strlen(data));
if(jsondecode(d, ev, &e) < 0)
	sysfatal("event: %r");
jsondecfree(d);

fmtstrinit(&f);
jsonenc(&j, &f);
jsonbegin(&j, '{');
jsonkey(&j, "model");
jsonstring(&j, "claude-opus-5-5");
jsonkey(&j, "max_tokens");
jsonint(&j, 32000);
jsonend(&j);
body = fmtstrflush(&f);
.EE
